/-!
# Locally principal closed subspaces and effective Cartier divisors on algebraic spaces

Let `X` be an algebraic space over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf site `B`, and let
`Z ⊆ X` be a closed subspace.  Its *defining ideal sheaf* `𝓘_Z ⊆ 𝒪_X` is the sheaf submodule
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealSheaf` of the structure sheaf on the small étale ringed
site of `X`.  `Z` is a *locally principal closed subspace* when `𝓘_Z` is locally generated by
one section, and an *effective Cartier divisor* when `𝓘_Z` is an invertible `𝒪_X`-module.

Both notions are conditions on `𝓘_Z` on the small étale site of `X`, the analogues of the
scheme-level `Stacks.Divisors.IsLocallyPrincipal` and `Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor`,
which impose the corresponding conditions on the ideal sheaf of a closed subscheme.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsLocallyPrincipal`: the defining ideal sheaf of the closed subspace
  is locally generated by one section.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor`: the defining ideal sheaf of the closed
  subspace is an invertible `𝒪_X`-module.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsPullbackDefined`: the pullback of a closed subspace along a
  morphism of algebraic spaces is defined when its inverse image is again an effective Cartier
  divisor.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsRelativeEffectiveCartierDivisor`: an effective Cartier divisor on
  `X` whose structure morphism to the base `Y` is flat.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.associatedInvertibleSheaf`: the invertible sheaf
  `𝒪_X(D) = 𝓘_D^{⊗ -1}`; `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.idealSheafHom` is its other description, the
  sheaf-hom `SheafHom_{𝒪_X}(𝓘_D, 𝒪_X)`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.canonicalSection`: the canonical global section `1_D` of `𝒪_X(D)`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.negativeDivisorSheaf`: `𝒪_X(-D) = 𝒪_X(D)^{⊗ -1}`, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.differenceDivisorSheaf`: `𝒪_X(D - D') = 𝒪_X(D) ⊗ 𝒪_X(-D')`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.associatedInvertibleSheaf_isInvertible`: `𝒪_X(D)` is invertible.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.associatedInvertibleSheafHomEquiv` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.associatedInvertibleSheafHomEquiv_apply`: the two descriptions of
  `𝒪_X(D)` agree, by a family of linear equivalences on sections natural for restriction and
  characterized by the evaluation pairing `𝒪_X(D) ⊗ 𝓘_D ⟶ 𝒪_X`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.canonicalSection_apply`: the canonical section corresponds, under
  that identification, to the inclusion `𝓘_D ⟶ 𝒪_X`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.negativeDivisorSheafIso`: the identification `𝒪_X(-D) ≅ 𝓘_D`.

## Implementation notes

The sections of `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealSheaf` over an étale scheme chart `U` of
`X` are `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt`, the kernel of `𝒪_X ⟶ i_*𝒪_Z` read on that
chart; by `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_eq_ker` that kernel is the kernel of pullback
of regular functions along the closed immersion of schemes `Z ×_X U ⟶ U`.

Quasi-coherence of `𝓘_Z` is not part of either statement: both conditions are on the ideal
sheaf being locally generated by one section, respectively invertible, and neither mentions
quasi-coherence, so `𝓘_Z` is taken as a plain sheaf submodule of `𝒪_X`.

The two descriptions of `𝒪_X(D)` have their sections in different universes.  The inverse
tensor power `𝓘_D^{⊗ -1}` has the sections of the structure sheaf, in `Type u`, whereas a section
of `SheafHom_{𝒪_X}(𝓘_D, 𝒪_X)` over an étale chart `U` is a morphism of `𝒪_X|_U`-modules on the
slice site over `U`, and those live in `Type (u + 1)`, the universe of the objects of the small
étale site.  So the two cannot be isomorphic as sheaves of modules: `𝒪_X(D)` is the inverse
tensor power, and its agreement with the sheaf-hom is a family of linear equivalences on
sections, natural for restriction and characterized by the evaluation pairing, given by
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.Invertible.tensorCurryLinearEquiv`.

Invertibility is `AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.IsInvertible`, stated through the universal
property of the tensor functor; the sheaf of rings indexing the modules is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.structureRingSheaf`, which is `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.ringSheaf`
of the small étale ringed site.

The inverse image in `IsPullbackDefined` is the pointwise preimage
`CategoryTheory.Subfunctor.preimage`, bundled as a closed subspace by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isClosedSubspace_preimage`; by
`CategoryTheory.Subfunctor.isPullback_preimage` it is the fibre product `D ×_X Y`.
`IsPullbackDefined` takes `D` to be an arbitrary closed subspace, not necessarily an effective
Cartier divisor.

In `IsRelativeEffectiveCartierDivisor` the structure morphism `D ⟶ Y` of a closed subspace
`D ⊆ X` over `f : X ⟶ Y` is the composite of the inclusion `D ⟶ X` with `f`, and flatness is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsFlat`.

